{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"EXT_ID:15060014 AND SRC:MED","internalQuery":"EXT_ID:15060014 AND SRC:MED","resultType":"CORE","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"15060014","source":"MED","pmid":"15060014","pmcid":"PMC383317","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC383317"]},"doi":"10.1101/gr.1933104","title":"Aligning multiple genomic sequences with the threaded blockset aligner.","authorString":"Blanchette M, Kent WJ, Riemer C, Elnitski L, Smit AF, Roskin KM, Baertsch R, Rosenbloom K, Clawson H, Green ED, Haussler D, Miller W.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Blanchette M","lastName":"Blanchette","firstName":"Mathieu","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Howard Hughes Medical Institute, University of California at Santa Cruz, Santa Cruz, California 95064, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Kent WJ","lastName":"Kent","firstName":"W James","initials":"WJ"},{"fullName":"Riemer C","lastName":"Riemer","firstName":"Cathy","initials":"C"},{"fullName":"Elnitski L","lastName":"Elnitski","firstName":"Laura","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1065-019X"}},{"fullName":"Smit AF","lastName":"Smit","firstName":"Arian F A","initials":"AF"},{"fullName":"Roskin KM","lastName":"Roskin","firstName":"Krishna M","initials":"KM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5864-5093"}},{"fullName":"Baertsch R","lastName":"Baertsch","firstName":"Robert","initials":"R"},{"fullName":"Rosenbloom K","lastName":"Rosenbloom","firstName":"Kate","initials":"K"},{"fullName":"Clawson H","lastName":"Clawson","firstName":"Hiram","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0480-0545"}},{"fullName":"Green ED","lastName":"Green","firstName":"Eric D","initials":"ED"},{"fullName":"Haussler D","lastName":"Haussler","firstName":"David","initials":"D"},{"fullName":"Miller W","lastName":"Miller","firstName":"Webb","initials":"W"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0480-0545"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5864-5093"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1065-019X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"14","journalIssueId":1136350,"dateOfPublication":"2004 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2004,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Genome Res","title":"Genome research","essn":"1549-5469","issn":"1088-9051","nlmid":"9518021","isoabbreviation":"Genome Res"},"printPublicationDate":"2004-04-01"},"pubYear":"2004","pageInfo":"708-715","abstractText":"We define a \"threaded blockset,\" which is a novel generalization of the classic notion of a multiple alignment. A new computer program called TBA (for \"threaded blockset aligner\") builds a threaded blockset under the assumption that all matching segments occur in the same order and orientation in the given sequences; inversions and duplications are not addressed. TBA is designed to be appropriate for aligning many, but by no means all, megabase-sized regions of multiple mammalian genomes. The output of TBA can be projected onto any genome chosen as a reference, thus guaranteeing that different projections present consistent predictions of which genomic positions are orthologous. This capability is illustrated using a new visualization tool to view TBA-generated alignments of vertebrate Hox clusters from both the mammalian and fish perspectives. Experimental evaluation of alignment quality, using a program that simulates evolutionary change in genomic sequences, indicates that TBA is more accurate than earlier programs. To perform the dynamic-programming alignment step, TBA runs a stand-alone program called MULTIZ, which can be used to align highly rearranged or incompletely sequenced genomes. We describe our use of MULTIZ to produce the whole-genome multiple alignments at the Santa Cruz Genome Browser.","affiliation":"Howard Hughes Medical Institute, Universi